# .gitignore for an infrastructure repository.
#
# Every rule says why it exists. A list of bare patterns is a list nobody can
# safely edit: the next person cannot tell which lines are load-bearing and
# which were cargo-culted, so they either leave dead rules forever or delete a
# live one. Rules that explain themselves get maintained.
#
# Copy this to .gitignore and keep the comments.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- plaintext
# The working set of secrets. Encrypted as secrets.tar.enc (see
# secrets-crypt.sh); the ciphertext is committed, these are not.
.env
*.env.local
secrets/

# Private keys and certificates, wherever they land.
*.pem
*.key
!*.pub

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ tooling
# Terraform state contains resolved secret values and provider credentials —
# it is as sensitive as the secrets themselves and belongs in a remote
# encrypted backend, never in git.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
# Templates stay tracked; they are the documentation of what must be filled in.
!*.tfvars.example

# Kubeconfigs embed client certificates or long-lived tokens.
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig
admin.conf

# Service account JSON keys.
sa-key*.json

# --------------------------------------------------- generated per-deployment
# Files below are produced by a bootstrap step and differ per deployment.
# Committing one deployment's copy overwrites everyone else's on the next
# pull — and because the file still looks plausible, the breakage surfaces
# later, somewhere else, as a configuration that "was working yesterday".
#
# This is not hypothetical: a stale placeholder file was once committed over a
# real one, and the failure showed up two environments away.
#
# Track the .example template instead. Anything a bootstrap generates from
# live state belongs here.
configs/generated/
**/bootstrap-*.json
**/containers.json

# ------------------------------------------------------- migration workspace
# Intermediate files from moving secrets into a secret manager: plaintext
# dumps of production values, rekey working files, one-shot credentials on
# their way into a CI settings page. All of them are short-lived and none of
# them should be one `git add -A` away from the history.
payloads/
**/payloads/
*-recon.json
.ci-vault-secrets.json

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- local
# Developer-local context and editor state; no secrets, just noise.
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
